.TH ARCH 1 2026-10-19 "w64devkit" "w64devkit Manual"
.SH NAME
arch \- print the processor architecture
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B arch
.RB [ \-\-help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B arch
prints the name of the processor architecture that busybox runs as,
followed by a newline, on standard output.
It is the same as
.BR "uname\ \-m" .
The 64-bit kit prints
.BR x86_64 ,
and the 32-bit kit prints
.BR i686 .
.PP
The name comes from the Windows
.B GetSystemInfo
function, which reports the architecture of the calling process, not the
machine's native one.
So on 64-bit Windows the 32-bit kit, which runs under WOW64, prints
.BR i686 ,
and on ARM64 Windows the 64-bit kit, which runs under x64 emulation,
prints
.BR x86_64 .
.SH OPTIONS
.B arch
has one option, and ignores its arguments, including unknown options and
operands, unless the first is
.BR \-\-help .
.TP
.B \-\-help
When it is the first argument, print a usage message on standard output
and exit with status 0, ignoring the other arguments.
In any other position it is ignored.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.B arch
reads no environment variables of its own.
Variables that affect every busybox applet are described in
.BR busybox (1).
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Standard output could not be written, as when it is closed or is a pipe
whose reader has exited.
A message is printed on standard error.
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the architecture:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ arch
x86_64
.EE
.RE
.PP
Create a build directory named after the architecture:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
$ mkdir \-p build/$(arch)
.EE
.RE
.SH NOTES
Unlike GNU
.BR arch ,
it has no
.B \-\-version
option, and it ignores operands and unknown options instead of rejecting
them.
.PP
The output line ends with LF, not CRLF.
.PP
The kit's
.B sh
runs
.B arch
inside the shell process, without starting a new one, except where the
shell needs a separate process anyway, as for a command substitution or
a pipeline.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR busybox (1),
.BR sh (1),
.BR uname (1)
